A||N||6||kornholio||removing rad cap||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||10-28-1999||08:18 PM||||When I removed the rad cap today, most of my coolant came pouring out.  It wasn't the pressure that was forcing the coolant out because I let all the pressure out before I removed the cap.  Is it normal to have the fluid gushing out like that or did someone overfill my car?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gokart||10-28-1999||08:26 PM||||How do you let the pressure out before removing the cap?||||reg||5
Z||000002||kornholio||10-28-1999||08:41 PM||||I turned the cap one notch and waited until the hissing stopped then I turned it all the way and removed the cap.  That's when all the coolant came out.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 10-28-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||10-28-1999||09:11 PM||||Are you talking about the cap on the radiator or the engine. The radiator is lower than the water level in the engine and water runs downhill.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kornholio||10-28-1999||09:41 PM||||so it's normal that that much coolant spilled out?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||10-28-1999||10:45 PM||||If you pull the radiator cap you should have a fair amount of coolant come out.  If you just want to check the level of the coolant pull the thermostat housing cap.  Shouldn't have any come out then.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||10-29-1999||10:13 AM||||The warmer the coolant is, the more it expands and the more likely it is to overflow when you remove the radiator cap.||||reg||1
